{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmAo5unsdqjh47bmG97m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0187UjwCmf2ZK4byckR2a8W5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex; python - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path(\"sec1_intro.tex\"); s=p.read_text()\na=\"open-stacks one, and the best exact search for the family came from it\\n\"\nassert a in s\ns=s.replace(a,\"open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\\\ref{sec:search} came\\n  from it\\n\")\np.write_text(s)\n\nm=Path(\"main.tex\"); t=m.read_text()\ni=t.index(\"\\\\begin{abstract}\"); j=t.index(\"\\\\end{abstract}\")\nnew=r\"\"\"\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nThe minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\nmake a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\ngraph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\nno proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\nresults, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\nthrough pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\neight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\npathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\nfalse as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\nSecond, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\ntimes as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\nrarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\ndominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\npathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\nand prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n\"\"\"\nt=t[:i]+new+t[j:]\nm.write_text(t)\nEOF\nsed -n '/begin{abstract}/,/end{abstract}/p' main.tex | wc -w"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1002,"cache_read_input_tokens":148812,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1002,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"458f789a-3218-4767-a875-babbaef1c57d","uuid":"c1ec6af5-f074-4adf-be99-21ba78f01683","timestamp":"2026-10-06T14:50:09.974Z","request_id":"req_011CfmAo5RXwWycFRdc44eKk","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0187UjwCmf2ZK4byckR2a8W5":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex; python - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path(\"sec1_intro.tex\"); s=p.read_text()\na=\"open-stacks one, and the best exact search for the family came from it\\n\"\nassert a in s\ns=s.replace(a,\"open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\\\ref{sec:search} came\\n  from it\\n\")\np.write_text(s)\n\nm=Path(\"main.tex\"); t=m.read_text()\ni=t.index(\"\\\\begin{abstract}\"); j=t.index(\"\\\\end{abstract}\")\nnew=r\"\"\"\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nThe minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\nmake a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\ngraph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\nno proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\nresults, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\nthrough pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\neight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\npathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\nfalse as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\nSecond, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\ntimes as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\nrarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\ndominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\npathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\nand prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n\"\"\"\nt=t[:i]+new+t[j:]\nm.write_text(t)\nEOF\nsed -n '/begin{abstract}/,/end{abstract}/p' main.tex | wc -w"}}}
